digraph ReasoningTree {
  rankdir="TB";
  "N1" [label="(1,0,0) Ferroelectrics have unique structural properties at unit cell level for various applications"];
  "N10" [label="(11,0,0) Ferroelectric catalysts unexpectedly show relatively poor water splitting performance in practice"];
  "N12" [label="(13,0,0) Internal screening charges naturally occur alongside crystal structure formation"];
  "N13" [label="(14,0,0) Partial elimination of internal screening charges moderately increases activity"];
  "N14" [label="(15,0,0) STO nanolayers on positive PTO surface enhance water-splitting activity by factor of 15"];
  "N15" [label="(17,0,0) Ferroelectrics have larger electric field for charge separation compared to other strategies"];
  "N16" [label="(18,0,0) Inherent driving force for charge separation in bulk phase is not an impediment to high activity"];
  "N17" [label="(19,0,0) Key question: what factors limit transfer of photogenerated charges to surface reaction sites?"];
  "N18" [label="(22,0,0) Main obstacle is Ti defects at positive polarization facets that inhibit electron transfer"];
  "N19" [label="(23,0,0) Eliminating Ti defects via STO nanolayers elevates AQE by 400x through efficient electron transfer route"];
  "N2" [label="(2,0,0) Symmetry-breaking electrostatic boundary condition generates permanent depolarization field, conferring unique physical properties"];
  "N22" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-knowledge: In photocatalysis, charge transfer occurs in two stages: bulk separation and surface transfer to reaction sites"];
  "N23" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-common: Surface modifications that improve charge transfer consistently enhance photocatalytic performance"];
  "N26" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-reasoning: Given paradox between theory and practice, and photocatalysis has bulk and surface stages, therefore the problem likely resides in surface charge transfer processes"];
  "N3" [label="(5,0,0) Studies explore coupling between ferroelectric polarization and light, including photocatalytic applications"];
  "N32" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-knowledge: Defect engineering is a general strategy in materials science for controlling electronic and catalytic properties"];
  "N4" [label="(6,0,0) Despite significant efforts, practical application of ferroelectrics faces significant challenges"];
  "N9" [label="(11,0,0) Theoretical electric field in ferroelectrics (~10^5 kV/cm) is 3-4 orders of magnitude higher than conventional semiconductors"];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) Develop and evaluate precise surface-defect-control strategies, especially SrTiO3-nanolayer elimination or passivation of Ti defects on positive-polarization PbTiO3 facets, to test whether improved photogenerated-electron transfer can overcome ferroelectric photocatalysis bottlenecks and enhance overall water-splitting performance.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) Converging evidence from a field/performance mismatch, surface-transfer bottleneck reasoning, surface-modification improvements, Ti-defect inhibition, and SrTiO3-nanolayer AQE gains motivates investigating precise surface-defect control in ferroelectric photocatalysis."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) Internal screening charges are a modifiable factor in ferroelectric photocatalysts, and their partial elimination is associated with a moderate activity increase."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) SrTiO3 nanolayers on the positive PbTiO3 surface constitute a surface modification associated with substantially enhanced water-splitting activity."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) If surface charge transfer is a likely bottleneck in ferroelectric photocatalysis, then surface modifications that improve charge transfer are plausible interventions for improving photocatalytic efficiency."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) Ti defects at positive polarization facets are implicated as a surface obstacle that can inhibit photogenerated-electron transfer to reaction sites."];
  "R15" [label="(0,0,0) The reported 400-fold AQE increase after eliminating Ti defects with SrTiO3 nanolayers is consistent with defect engineering controlling electron-transfer pathways and photocatalytic performance."];
  "R16" [label="(0,0,0) Ferroelectric structural and polarization-related properties motivate their consideration as photocatalysts for light-driven charge separation."];
  "R17" [label="(0,0,0) The permanent depolarization field in ferroelectrics provides a bulk-stage feature relevant to separating photogenerated charges before surface transfer occurs."];
  "R18" [label="(0,0,0) Prior work coupling ferroelectric polarization with light supports considering ferroelectrics for photocatalytic solar-energy conversion applications."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) Ferroelectrics possess unusually large internal/theoretical electric fields that can provide a strong driving force for charge separation relative to conventional semiconductor strategies."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) Despite their proposed advantages, ferroelectric photocatalysts are reported to show poor or challenging practical water-splitting performance compared with other strategies."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) Ferroelectric photocatalysts therefore present a contrast between strong internal charge-separation fields and disappointing practical water-splitting performance."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) The contrast between strong ferroelectric charge-separation fields and poor practical activity supports treating the field/performance mismatch as a real bottleneck problem rather than as a lack of bulk driving force alone."];
